# Hover Racer directional thrusters

Reopen the project after input-map changes, then run Main with F6. Preserve any unsaved editor work separately before reloading external edits.

Both sticks point the ship toward the requested direction across the full circle, then drive it forward along its heading. Up means ahead; left/right means turn the nose left/right and travel that way; down means turn around and travel behind. Down no longer means nose-fixed reverse. Magnitude controls thrust with a radial deadzone of 0.18. Trigger propulsion and the previous small horizontal steering assist are removed.

The heading reference is captured from the ship/follow camera when a gesture begins. It stays fixed while either stick remains deflected: holding left reaches a leftward heading and stays there, even as the camera follows the turn. Centre BOTH sticks to coast and refresh the reference; the next up gesture means forward from the new facing. Moving a held stick around its circle updates the target within that fixed frame.

The two independent stick vectors blend to a target direction. One active stick supplies half the combined propulsion. Near-opposite inputs blend into differential turning instead of following an unstable near-zero target: left up/right down spins right, inverse spins left. Equal opposed horizontal inputs cancel. Neutral cuts powered turning/thrust and glides in the existing direction while slowing at 13 m/s², reaching rest from 52 m/s in about four seconds. Opposed active thrusters still preserve moving drift while spinning. Powered grip redirects movement toward the nose. Bank, stern swing and skipping remain visual only.

Keyboard equivalents: WASD is the left stick; arrow keys are the right stick. A+Left commands left; D+Right commands right; W+Up drives ahead; S+Down turns around and drives behind. W+Down spins right; S+Up spins left. Y/R resets. Individual exhaust indicates each engine's strength; cyan rear exhaust is forward drive, orange front exhaust shows opposed counter-thrust.

The saved course remains approximately 918 metres long and 28 metres wide, with six rounded corners, a dogleg, and hills rising 10 and 14 metres. Hover suspension samples actual road collisions, with tiny fallback samples for triangle-edge ray misses. Falling off the course resets the ship.

Tunable Ship exports: top speed 52 m/s, acceleration 145 m/s², neutral coast deceleration 13 m/s², bounded turn speed 1.8 rad/s, heading response 6, bank up to 36 degrees, thrust grip 6.5, hover response 22. Speed and acceleration increased from 40 and 110. FOV changes smoothly by at most 8 degrees; mesh animation does not tilt/bob the camera or collider.

Run the checks from this project directory:

```
Godot --headless --fixed-fps 60 --path . --script res://tests/controller_input.gd
Godot --headless --fixed-fps 60 --path . --script res://tests/hill_course.gd
```

The control test uses simulated controller events and checks actual facing and travel on a large isolated collision floor. It covers held directions, direction changes without recentering, reference refresh, mixed/opposed vectors, radial analog response, coast, keyboard, reset and cap. The hill test checks the actual course, barriers and full-speed hill following under power. Neither judges physical controller feel or visual comfort.

`tools/build_course.gd` regenerates TestTrack.tscn and course_samples.json, replacing manual course edits. Back up before running it. Files before this control update are preserved in `backups/before-full-circle-heading` with `.bak` suffixes.

## Canyon and twin-engine ship

The original low-poly ship now has separated gold/blue engine pods, a small trailing cockpit, tethers, an energy linkage, independent rotating intake blades and stronger exhaust. Its collision box is 3.1 × 0.4 × 4.6 metres. The chase camera is higher/farther back to frame the whole ship; thrust adds a smooth pullback of at most 0.55 metres and returns on release. FOV remains capped at the existing additional 8 degrees, with no camera shake, roll or bob.

CanyonSlice.tscn is independent scenery on the east straight: close faceted cliffs, repeated lit posts, ground rocks and three overhead arches, ending before the corner approach. All geometry is original and local; `tools/build_canyon.gd` regenerates only the scenery. The existing course generator remains unchanged.

Original synthesized engine and wind WAV loops are in `audio/`. Engine audio is currently disabled (assets retained). Wind follows actual horizontal speed at a subdued level, continues during the slowing glide and stops at rest. Reset stops all audio and restores the camera. Tune volume in `ship.gd` if needed; physical loudness and comfort need an in-game check.

Additional automated validation:

```
Godot --headless --fixed-fps 60 --path . --script res://tests/canyon_podracer.gd
```

Pre-update files are in `backups/before-canyon-podracer` with `.bak` suffixes. Full-circle controls, 52 m/s speed cap and 145 m/s² acceleration are unchanged.

## Neutral slowing and engine mute

Both sticks released now applies predictable passive slowing, without changing heading or the direction of drift. Top speed remains 52 and acceleration 145. Engine audio cannot play; the original files are retained. Subdued wind remains tied to actual speed. The HUD now says “glide + slow stop.”

```
Godot --headless --fixed-fps 60 --path . --script res://tests/neutral_coast.gd
```

This checks a four-second stop from top speed, no acceleration or power from neutral/deadzone noise, powered launch, visual exhaust release, muted engines, wind at speed/rest and reset. Pre-change backups are in `backups/before-coast-slowdown`.
